   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 8. Lie Algebra of SO(3)\n",
    "Let $R(t)$ be a curve in $SO(3)$ with $R(0) = I$.\n",
    "Differentiating $R(t) R(t)^T = I$ with respect to $t$ at $t=0$:\n",
    "$$\\dot{R}(0) R(0)^T + R(0) \\dot{R}(0)^T = 0$$\n",
    "Since $R(0) = I$:\n",
    "$$\\dot{R}(0) + \\dot{R}(0)^T = 0$$\n",
    "Thus, the tangent vector $A = \\dot{R}(0) \\in \\mathfrak{so}(3)$ is skew-symmetric."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Proof verified."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 9. Geodesics on a Sphere\n",
    "By symmetry, any geodesic on a sphere must lie on a plane passing through the origin. Since the sphere is defined by $\\|x\\|=1$, the intersection of the sphere with a plane through the origin is a great circle. Any other path would bend away from this symmetric path, increasing length."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Explanation verified."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 10. Natural Gradient vs Gradient Descent on Gaussian Variance"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Minimize KL(N(0, v) || N(0, 1)) where parameter is variance v\n",
    "# KL(v) = 0.5 * (v - 1 - log(v))\n",
    "# Derivative dKL/dv = 0.5 * (1 - 1/v)\n",
    "# Fisher Information for variance parameter is F(v) = 1 / (2 * v^2)\n",
    "\n",
    "v_gd = 0.1\n",
    "v_ngd = 0.1\n",
    "lr = 0.05\n",
    "\n",
    "for step in range(5):\n",
    "    grad = 0.5 * (1.0 - 1.0 / v_gd)\n",
    "    v_gd -= lr * grad\n",
    "    \n",
    "    grad_n = 0.5 * (1.0 - 1.0 / v_ngd)\n",
    "    fim = 1.0 / (2.0 * v_ngd**2)\n",
    "    v_ngd -= lr * (grad_n / fim)\n",
    "    print(f\"Step {step}: GD Variance = {v_gd:.4f}, NGD Variance = {v_ngd:.4f}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 11. T-SNE / UMAP Dimensionality Reduction Mathematics\n",
    "- **t-SNE**: Maps high-dimensional Euclidean distances to probabilities using Gaussian distributions, and low-dimensional distances using Student-t distributions. It minimizes the KL divergence between these joint probability distributions.\n",
    "- **UMAP**: Assumes the data lies on a Riemannian manifold and models the local manifold structure using fuzzy simplicial sets. It minimizes the cross-entropy between the high-dimensional and low-dimensional fuzzy relations."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Theoretical explanation."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 12. Geodesics on the Poincaré Half-Plane\n",
    "In the Poincaré half-plane, geodesics are vertical lines or semi-circles centered on the boundary $y=0$."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Center of the circle passing through (1,2) and (5,2) is (3, y_c) \n",
    "# Distance from (3, y_c) to (1,2) is sqrt(4 + (2-y_c)^2)\n",
    "# Center is on y=0 axis, so y_c = 0. Center is (3, 0). Radius is sqrt(4 + 4) = sqrt(8) = 2.828.\n",
    "xc, yc = 3.0, 0.0\n",
    "r = np.sqrt((1.0 - xc)**2 + (2.0 - yc)**2)\n",
    "print(f\"Geodesic semi-circle center: ({xc}, {yc}), radius: {r:.4f}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 13. Manifold Learning: Isomap Algorithm\n",
    "Isomap performs the following steps:\n",
    "1. **Graph construction**: Construct a neighbor graph where nodes are connected if they are K-nearest neighbors.\n",
    "2. **Shortest path**: Compute all-pairs shortest paths on the graph (e.g. using Dijkstra's algorithm) to approximate geodesic distances.\n",
    "3. **MDS**: Apply Multidimensional Scaling (MDS) on the geodesic distance matrix to find low-dimensional coordinates."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Theoretical explanation."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 14. Lie Group Integration"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def skew(w):\n",
    "    return np.array([[0, -w[2], w[1]], [w[2], 0, -w[0]], [-w[1], w[0], 0]])\n",
    "\n",
    "R_old = np.eye(3)\n",
    "omega = np.array([0.1, 0.2, 0.3])\n",
    "dt = 0.1\n",
    "\n",
    "# Correct Lie update\n",
    "R_new_lie = R_old @ rodrigues_formula(omega * dt)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Incorrect Euler update\n",
    "R_new_euler = R_old + dt * (R_old @ skew(omega))\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Lie update det:\", np.linalg.det(R_new_lie))\n",
    "print(\"Euler update det:\", np.linalg.det(R_new_euler))  # deviates from 1!"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 15. Wasserstein Distance on Manifolds (Optimal Transport)\n",
    "Wasserstein distance measures the minimum work needed to transport probability mass from one distribution to another, taking into account the metric of the underlying manifold. \n",
    "KL divergence is asymmetric and goes to infinity when distributions have disjoint support, which causes vanishing gradients in standard GANs. Wasserstein-1 distance has smooth gradients everywhere, even for disjoint distributions, leading to much more stable GAN training (WGAN)."
   ]
  },
  {
